Referral Criteria 
Refer patients who fail to improve, or get worse, despite therapy for 
acute diarrhoea. Refer all patients with chronic diarrhoea to a specialist 
for further evaluation and management.
Treatment algorithm
Fluid management for children with diarrhoea
Treatment Plan A – No dehydration
yy
Child can be treated safely at home
yy
Instruct mother to give home-based fluids like rice water, koko, soup, 

— Diarrhoea —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
16
water, and Oral Rehydration Salt (ORS). 
yy
Breastfed babies should be given breast milk and ORS
yy
Give as much as child wants of all the fluids
yy
Child should continue to feed
yy
Ask the mother to return to the health facility if the child gets worse, 
passes more watery stools, vomits repeatedly, becomes very thirsty, 
eats or drinks poorly or is not better in 2 days
yy
Instruct mother on how to prevent diarrhoea
ORS currently recommended for use in mild to moderate diarrhoea 
has a reduced sodium and glucose concentration (low osmolarity).
How to prepare ORS
ORS: Dissolve the contents of one sachet of ORS in 600 ml or 1000 
mls depending on type of ORS.
yy
To get 600 ml, use 2 small (300 ml) soft drink bottles or 1 big beer 
bottle 
yy
To get 1000 ml, use 1L mineral water bottle 
The child or adult should drink AS MUCH of it as he/she wants. If the 
child vomits, the mother should wait about 10 minutes and give it again.
Table 1-2:  Treatment by Fluid Therapy - Plan A
