12.	 Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease
Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD) is caused by backflow of 
gastric and/or duodenal contents past the lower oesophageal sphincter 
into the oesophagus without belching or vomiting.
The disease is classified into two groups based on endoscopy findings 
as non-erosive gastro-oesophageal disease (non-erosive GORD) and 
erosive gastro-oesophageal disease (erosive GORD). Failure to treat may 
result in oesophagitis, ulceration, strictures and rarely adenocarcinoma.
Causes 
yy
Obesity
yy
Hiatus hernia
yy
Increased intra-abdominal pressure e.g. in pregnancy
yy
Long term use of nasogastric tube
yy
Agents that decrease lower oesophageal sphincter pressure e.g. 
alcohol, cigarettes, anticholinergics (e.g. Hyoscine butylbromide, 
Propantheline bromide), other drugs – Morphine, Diazepam, 
Pethidine and Calcium channel blockers
yy
Children with chronic neurological disease (e.g. cerebral palsy)
Symptoms
yy
Heartburn (worsens with vigorous exercise, bending forward, lying; 
relieved by antacids and sitting upright)
yy
Dyspepsia
yy
Early satiety
yy
Retrosternal and epigastric pain (mimics angina pectoris by radiating 
to neck, jaws and arms. The pain is worse on bending down e.g. 
sweeping)
yy
Pain on swallowing
yy
Difficulty swallowing
yy
Nocturnal regurgitation (wakes patients up with coughing, choking 
and filling of the mouth with ‘saliva’)
yy
Asthma-like (may be worse at night)
In children
yy
Failure to thrive/refusing food
yy
Vomiting
yy
Coughing 
yy
Forceful regurgitation which may lead to aspiration pneumonia
yy
Iron deficiency anaemia 
yy
Wheezing
 
Signs
yy
May be none

