('7','Haemorrhoids','1','Disorders of the Gastrointestinal Tract  ................................','29','29-32','yy Passage of bright red blood at defaecation yy Not mixed with stools yy May spray the toilet bowl or only found on the toilet paper after cleaning yy Mucoid discharge yy Swelling at anus yy Perianal irritation or itch (pruritus ani) yy Discomfort after opening bowels yy Anal pain (occurs during an acute attack of prolapse with thrombosis, congestion and oedema) yy Symptoms of anaemia','yy May be none (inspection of the anus and digital rectal examination may be normal) yy Redundant folds of skin (skin tags) seen in the position of the haemorrhoids. Straining may show the haemorrhoids yy Swelling at the anus (in third degree haemorrhoids) yy Palpable thrombosed internal haemorrhoids on rectal examination yy Signs of complications (profuse bleeding with anaemia or haemorrhagic shock, prolapse, strangulation, thrombosis, infection or ulceration) yy Pallor','yy FBC yy Proctoscopy (the gold standard for diagnosis) yy Sigmoidoscopy (to exclude carcinoma of rectum)','Treatment objectives yy To correct anaemia, if present yy To relieve symptoms yy To prevent complications Non-pharmacological treatment yy Increase intake of fluid and roughage yy Avoid prolonged straining at defecation yy For prolapsed haemorrhoids, lie patient down and elevate the foot end of the bed. Try gentle digital reduction after application of local anaesthetic cream. If this fails, apply cold compresses. Sedation of the patient may be required yy For infected haemorrhoids, warm sitz baths 2-3 times a day yy Surgical treatment: — Haemorrhoids — Chapter 1: Disorders of the Gastrointestinal Tract 31 yy Rubber band ligation for second-degree haemorrhoids. yy Haemorrhoidectomy for third degree haemorrhoids. yy Haemorrhoids developing during pregnancy should be man­ aged conservatively as most will resolve after delivery Pharmacological treatment A. When associated with constipation Evidence 