38.	 Neonatal conjunctivitis  
Neonatal conjunctivitis or ophthalmia neonatorum is an acute 
purulent conjunctivitis during the first month of life. It is usually contracted 
from infected genital secretions of the mother. 
Cleaning of the neonate’s eyes immediately after birth and the 
application of 1% tetracycline ointment into the eyes, is effective in 
preventing the condition. This must be implemented as a policy in all 
health facilities in which child deliveries are undertaken.
Causes 
yy
Neisseria gonorrhoea
yy
Chlamydia trachomatis
yy
Staphylococci
yy
Streptococci
yy
Herpes Simplex  virus
yy
Chemical e.g. silver nitrate 
Symptoms
yy
Eye discharge
yy
Swelling of the eye lids
Signs
yy
Eye discharge, which may be purulent
yy
Redness and swelling of the conjunctivae
yy
Oedema and redness of the eyelids
Investigations
yy
Conjunctival swabs for Gram staining and culture 
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To treat the infection
yy
To prevent blindness 
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Clean the eyelids frequently (every 2 hours) with cotton wool dipped 
in sterile saline solution. In the absence of sterile saline solution, use 
boiled water that has been left to cool

